From ed93177dc31fa2a84ddd90c4f5248be4249d4de7 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Jonathan Barnes <hello@jonbarnes.dev>
Date: Thu, 24 Sep 2026 07:30:27 -0400
Subject: [PATCH] Bounce light, reflections on water and the air catching light

Patch 0007 of the renderer series (OoT: Recompiled, .scaffold/upgrades/lighting-design.md).

Level 3 of the row. The bounce pass sends one cosine weighted ray per pixel and takes the light
arriving from what it hits as the light bounced onto the surface, which the direct pass adds to
the game's ambient through the surface's own color. The reflection pass takes the water the
primary probe found (a horizontal translucent surface in front of the opaque hit; translucent
draws have a query mask of their own since patch 0006) and reflects one ray off it, weighted by
Fresnel. The volumetric pass marches the eye's ray in a few jittered steps and sums the sun
reaching the air (a shadow ray per step, under a sky only) and each local light's glow at the
game's own falloff.

The light arriving from a point is the frame's own color where the point is on screen and is
what the frame shows there, the game's lit vertex color at the hit (interpolated from the RSP's
shaded colors) scaled by what a texture takes on average where it is not, and the pixel's fog
color where the ray left the scene, which is the game's own horizon.

The composite gains its second output: the reflections and the scatter are light added to the
frame, which a factor cannot carry, so the compose shader writes them to a second target and a
second post process draw adds them onto the color target with an additive blend, after the
multiply.

Pinned edits, each marked "patch 0007": a visualization mode; the scatter texture in the common
set, the compose set and the shader's register block; the compose pipeline's second target and
the additive post process pipeline; the bounce dispatch before the direct pass and the scatter
dispatch after it with their timer spans, the reflection dispatch timed; the compose barriers and
the second post process draw; the debug view.

